WEBVTT

00:08:29.077 --> 00:08:31.244
you're getting them there,
one way or another.

00:08:31.268 --> 00:08:33.664
Before long, you find yourself
staring at a turtle,

00:08:33.688 --> 00:08:35.871
and asking yourself, "Is this a puzzle?"

00:08:35.895 --> 00:08:37.537
(Laughter)

00:08:37.561 --> 00:08:39.553
And also, staring at a turtle and saying,

00:08:39.577 --> 00:08:43.106
"I never appreciated what multitudes
this contains in its shell alone."

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:45.656
This might be
a familiar experience to you,

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:49.434
if you've ever been watching a TED Talk
and asked yourself, "Is this a puzzle?"

00:08:49.458 --> 00:08:50.910
(Laughter)

00:08:50.934 --> 00:08:52.322
I'm not telling.

00:08:52.346 --> 00:08:53.570
But what I will say

00:08:53.594 --> 00:08:57.138
is that puzzles can be found
in the most unexpected of places.

00:08:57.569 --> 00:09:00.490
That brings us back to one
of my favorite puzzles of all time,